# Run in a container

Pre-built containers with {{repo_name}} and its dependencies already
installed are available on [Github Container Registry](https://ghcr.io/{{github_org}}/{{repo_name}}).

## Starting the container

Pre-built images are published to the container registry on every release.
{% if project_type == 'web_api' %}
The image runs the FastAPI app with uvicorn; the simplest way to start it
with the local Postgres is the compose stack:

```
$ docker compose -f compose.local.yml up --build
```

The API is then on http://localhost:8000 (Swagger UI at `/docs`, health
probe at `/health`). To run the image directly (you supply the database):

```
$ docker run --rm -p 8000:8000 ghcr.io/{{github_org | lower}}/{{repo_name}}:latest
```
{% else %}To pull the container from github container registry and run:

```
$ docker run ghcr.io/{{github_org | lower}}/{{repo_name}}:latest --version
```
{% endif %}
To get a released version, use a numbered release instead of `latest`.

{% if mcp_effective %}
## Running the MCP server in the container

The image ships the `mcp-server-{{ repo_name }}` console script alongside the
CLI. To serve the MCP server over Streamable HTTP:

```
$ docker run --rm -p 8000:8000 \
    -e MCP_ALLOWED_HOSTS=localhost,127.0.0.1 \
    --entrypoint mcp-server-{{ repo_name }} {{ repo_name }} \
    --transport streamable-http --host 0.0.0.0
```

`MCP_ALLOWED_HOSTS` is required for the non-local bind: the MCP SDK only
arms its DNS-rebinding protection for localhost, so `mcp_server.py` refuses
to start without an explicit host allowlist (it rejects every request the
SDK does not know). Health check for the container orchestrator:
`curl http://localhost:8000/health` (unauthenticated; custom routes bypass
the allowlist by design, and expose no tools or data).

If your task runner is configured for it, the same command is one task:
`just mcp-serve` / `task mcp-serve` / `poe mcp-serve`.
{% endif %}

## Logging in the cloud

Logs go to stderr as human-readable console text by default; set the
`LOG_FORMAT=json` environment variable to switch to one-JSON-object-per-line
instead, which log aggregators (CloudWatch Logs, Cloud Logging, Azure
Monitor, journald, ...) can parse. This is independent of which (if any)
cloud SDK dependencies were added, and of which platform runs the
container — the same image works locally (console) and in any cloud
(JSON), so there's nothing extra to build per target: just set the
environment variable wherever the container runs.

[Render](https://render.com/docs/logging), [Railway](https://docs.railway.com/guides/structured-logging-production)
and [Vercel](https://vercel.com/kb/guide/add-structured-application-logs-to-vercel-functions)
all colour-code and filter by severity in their log viewer if the JSON has a
`level` + `message` field pair; `logging_setup.py`'s JSON mode already uses
`level` but names the message field `event` (structlog's own convention), so
those three won't pick up severity colouring out of the box. If that
matters to you, swap `"event"` for `"message"` in `logging_setup.py` — logs
are fully readable either way, this only affects that colouring/filtering.
